## 1. Executive summary

This project asked where a mid-size language model represents *which side of a threshold a bet has
made favourable*, and whether editing that representation changes what it estimates. It found the
representation, failed to make it causal at the activation level in three independent attempts,
and then showed — by intervening on the text instead — that the belief really is driving the
behaviour. **The gap between those two answers is the result.**

**The behaviour is overt, not covert [measured].** In `{{lf_model_id}}`, {{w3_mention_k}} of
{{w3_mention_n}} incentive traces ({{w3_mention_pct}} %) name the bet, the threshold or the cause
in the visible text, against about {{lf_covert_pct}} % spontaneous disclosure in the predecessor
study's {{lf_panel_size}}B panel. This model does not leak a preference; it announces one. *The
cross-scale reading is* **[suggested]** *— two model families, no sweep.*

**The behaviour is also fragile [measured].** The landing gap that selected this model at
screening — {{w2_gap_q25_judge_gt}}, {{lf_ci_pct}} % interval
[{{w2_ci_lo_q25}}, {{w2_ci_hi_q25}}], at n = {{w2_n_side}} per side — collapsed to
{{w3_gap_A_judge_gt}}, interval [{{w3_ci_lo_A}}, {{w3_ci_hi_A}}], at n = {{w3_n_side}} per side
under byte-identical apparatus (z = {{wc_z}}, two-sided p = {{wc_p}}). A gate that promotes the
largest of several noisy estimates promotes the one luck inflated most, and this is that effect
measured end to end.

**The aggregate gap hides a decomposition [measured].** Traces whose text gets the mapping right
show gaps of {{w3_cond_gap_A}} and {{w3_cond_gap_B}} on the two surface forms; traces that get it
wrong show {{w3_condnot_gap_A}} and {{w3_condnot_gap_B}}. Comprehension is itself asymmetric — the
arm where *exceeding* the threshold is favoured is understood {{w3_correct_A_above}} % of the time
against {{w3_correct_B_below}}–{{w3_correct_A_below}} % for its mirror — so the aggregate is a
comprehension-weighted mixture of two large opposite effects, not a small effect.

**The believed side is linearly decodable before it is spoken [measured].** A probe on the
residual stream at the model's own intermediate-estimate positions recovers the believed favoured
side at balanced accuracy {{w5_B_balacc_lstar}} against a null {{lf_ci_pct}}th percentile of
{{w5_B_null_lstar}} (p = {{w5_B_p_lstar}}), significant at {{w5_B_nsig}} of {{w5_n_layers}} layers
on the reskin. {{lf_w5_before_B}} of {{lf_w5_total_B}} of the carrying points fall **before** the
trace first names a cause. The mirrored form is flat at every layer with working positive
controls; at {{w5_A_minority}} minority-class traces that is a power limit, not an absence, and
neither is claimed.

**Writing the direction back in does not steer the belief [measured null].** Injecting
±α·‖Δμ‖·û at every generated position, at six doses from {{d_a025_pct}} % to {{d_a4_pct}} % of the
residual-stream norm and against {{n_null_directions}} matched random directions, produced **no
direction-specific landing effect at any dose**: the pre-registered primary contrast moved the
wrong way and beat {{w7_dplus_beats}} of {{n_w7_nulls}} random directions (p = {{w7_dplus_p}}).
Large doses do move behaviour hard — at α = {{alpha_max}} the median estimate falls from
{{w7_medlog_sham}} to {{w7_medlog_ap4}} on a log scale and landing from {{w7_P_sham}} to
{{w7_P_ap4}} — but that effect survives deleting the bet, does not mirror, and is matched by
random directions of equal norm.

**But the belief does drive the behaviour — through the text [measured].** Wave 2 attacked the
same question from the side an activation edit cannot reach. Rewriting the bet note so the model
understands it *better* raised comprehension to {{w11_pa}} and moved the landing gap to
{{w11_gap}} [{{w11_gap_lo}}, {{w11_gap_hi}}] — inside the {{w11_pred}} [{{w11_pred_lo}},
